"""Phase 4 copy, batch C: race time predictor, running pace, rucking, workout timer. Original prose (ADR 0036)."""
from phase4_common import base, emit, s

D = {}

D["race-time-predictor"] = base(
    "race-time-predictor",
    title="Race time predictor",
    h1="Race time predictor",
    description="Predict your 1500 m, mile, 5K, 10K, half marathon and marathon times from one recent race, using Riegel's formula, with pace per km and per mile.",
    intro="Enter a recent race distance and time to predict your equivalent times from 1500 m to the marathon. Each row shows the predicted time and the pace per kilometre and per mile to aim for.",
    q="How do I predict my race time at another distance?",
    a=["Apply Riegel's formula, T2 = T1 × (D2 ÷ D1)^1.06, with T1 the time you ran over distance D1. A 25:00 5K predicts a 10K in 52:07, a half marathon in 1:55:00 and a marathon in 3:59:47.",
       "The exponent of 1.06 accounts for slowing down over longer distances. Predictions far beyond the race you entered are a best case, not a guarantee."],
    sections=[
        s("Riegel's formula",
          "In 1977, the American engineer and runner Peter Riegel published a simple equation that described how race times grow with distance. He noticed that, across many sports and distances, time rose a little faster than distance. Doubling the distance more than doubled the time, by a consistent amount. His formula, T2 = T1 × (D2 ÷ D1)^1.06, captures that pattern with a single number, the exponent 1.06.",
          "Riegel later refined the work in a 1981 paper in American Scientist, comparing world records in running, swimming, cycling and walking. The 1.06 exponent fitted running records between about 3.5 minutes and 4 hours remarkably well. It has since become the most widely used race prediction formula, built into many calculators and training apps."),
        s("How the exponent works",
          "If the exponent were 1.0, you would hold the same pace at every distance, and a 25:00 5K would predict a 50:00 10K. The exponent of 1.06 adds a little extra time for each increase in distance. Doubling the distance multiplies the time by 2^1.06, about 2.085, so the 10K prediction becomes 52:07.",
          ("table", ["Distance ratio", "Time multiplier"], [["2× (5K to 10K)", "2.085"], ["4.22× (5K to half)", "4.60"], ["8.44× (5K to marathon)", "9.59"], ["2× (half to marathon)", "2.085"]]),
          "Some coaches use a slightly higher exponent, around 1.07 or 1.08, for less experienced runners, who tend to slow more over long distances. The calculator uses Riegel's original 1.06."),
        s("Worked examples",
          "From a 25:00 5K, the predictions are:",
          ("table", ["Distance", "Predicted time", "Pace /km", "Pace /mi"], [["1500 m", "6:59", "4:39", "7:29"], ["1 mile", "7:31", "4:40", "7:31"], ["3K", "14:33", "4:51", "7:48"], ["5K", "25:00 (your time)", "5:00", "8:03"], ["10K", "52:07", "5:13", "8:23"], ["Half marathon", "1:55:00", "5:27", "8:46"], ["Marathon", "3:59:47", "5:41", "9:09"]]),
          "From a 50:00 10K, the predictions are a 23:59 5K, a 1:50:19 half marathon and a 3:50:01 marathon. Notice that the 10K runner's predicted 5K is a minute faster than the first runner's actual 5K, consistent with the 10K being a stronger performance."),
        s("Why predictions get less accurate with distance",
          "Riegel's formula assumes that you are equally well trained for every distance. In practice, that is rarely true. A runner who trains for 5K races may have excellent speed but limited endurance; their marathon prediction assumes endurance they haven't built. Most recreational runners run slower in a marathon than a 5K-based prediction suggests, often by 5 to 10% or more.",
          "The marathon also brings challenges that shorter races don't: fuelling, hydration, glycogen depletion and the muscular fatigue of running for three or four hours. A 10K or half marathon result predicts a marathon much better than a 5K does. For the most reliable prediction, use a race no more than about four times shorter than your target."),
        s("Equivalent times, not targets",
          "The predictions are best read as equivalent performances: the same fitness expressed across different distances. They show what a well-prepared runner of your fitness might achieve, given training for that distance. They are not a promise of what you will run if you enter a race tomorrow.",
          "Coaches use equivalent times in the same way as Jack Daniels' VDOT tables or similar systems: to compare races, set realistic goals and choose training paces. If your predicted marathon is 3:59:47 but you have never run more than 15 km, a first marathon target of around 4:15 to 4:30 may be more realistic, with the faster time as a longer-term goal."),
        s("Using predictions to set training paces",
          "Your predicted times give useful training paces. Threshold runs are often done at roughly half marathon to 10K pace, VO2 max intervals at around 3K to 5K pace, and easy runs well slower than marathon pace. From a 25:00 5K, that suggests threshold work around 5:13 to 5:27 per km, intervals around 4:51 to 5:00 per km, and easy running from about 6:15 per km or slower.",
          "As fitness improves, update the input race and the paces with it. Racing or time-trialling every 6 to 12 weeks keeps the numbers current."),
        s("Other prediction methods",
          ("ul", [
              "**VDOT (Jack Daniels):** tables based on oxygen cost, giving equivalent times and training paces.",
              "**Cameron's formula:** an alternative curve fitted to elite and age-group records.",
              "**Marathon-specific models:** some use weekly mileage or long-run history alongside a shorter race to predict the marathon more realistically.",
              "**Age-grading:** adjusts times for age and sex using world-record standards, useful for comparing performances.",
          ]),
          "For most distances from 1500 m to the half marathon, these methods give results close to Riegel. They diverge most for the marathon, where training history matters most."),
        s("Getting a reliable input",
          "The prediction is only as good as the race you enter. Use a recent, all-out effort on an accurately measured course, run in reasonable conditions. A parkrun or certified road race works well. A training run, a race on a hilly course or a race in heat will give slower predictions than your true fitness.",
          "If you have several recent results, enter each and compare. If a 10K predicts a faster half marathon than your 5K does, your endurance may be relatively strong; if the 5K predicts faster, your speed may be the stronger quality. That information can shape your training."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How accurate are race time predictions?", "For distances close to your input race, often within a few per cent. For much longer races, especially the marathon, predictions are usually optimistic unless you have trained for that distance."),
        ("What is Riegel's formula?", "T2 = T1 × (D2 ÷ D1)^1.06. It predicts a time at a new distance from a known time, adding extra time for longer races."),
        ("What will my marathon time be from my 5K?", "From a 25:00 5K, Riegel predicts 3:59:47. Most recreational runners run slower in their first marathon, so treat it as a best case."),
        ("Which race should I use for a marathon prediction?", "A half marathon or 10K predicts the marathon much better than a 5K or mile."),
        ("Why is my predicted pace slower for longer races?", "Pace naturally falls with distance as fatigue builds. The 1.06 exponent accounts for this."),
        ("Can I use a training run?", "It's better to use a race or time trial at full effort. Training runs usually give slower predictions than your true fitness."),
        ("Can I use an AI assistant to plan from my predictions?", "Yes, to draft a training plan for your coach. Share your race, predictions, weekly mileage and target race, and ask for realistic goals."),
    ],
    prompt=dict(
        heading="Use your result with ChatGPT",
        when="After entering a recent race and reviewing your predicted times.",
        paste="Paste your race result, predictions and training history.",
        text="I recently ran [distance] in [time]. Riegel's formula predicts [10K time], [half marathon time] and [marathon time]. I currently run [weekly distance] over [number] sessions a week, with a longest run of [long run]. My target race is [race] on [date]. Explain how realistic the prediction is for my target, given my training. Suggest a goal time, the paces I should use for easy runs, threshold sessions and intervals, and the main changes my training needs before race day. List questions for my coach.",
    ),
    nxt=[("Running pace calculator", "/tools/running-pace-calculator"), ("VO2 max calculator", "/tools/vo2-max-calculator"), ("Heart rate zone calculator", "/tools/heart-rate-zone-calculator")],
)

D["running-pace-calculator"] = base(
    "running-pace-calculator",
    title="Running pace calculator",
    h1="Running pace calculator",
    description="Work out your running pace per km and per mile, and your speed in km/h and mph, from any distance and time. See finish times at that pace for common races.",
    intro="Type in how far you ran and how long it took to get your pace per kilometre and per mile, your speed, and the finish times you would run at that pace over 5K, 10K, the half marathon and the marathon.",
    q="How do I calculate my running pace?",
    a="Divide your time by the distance. Running 10 km in 50:00 is 50 minutes ÷ 10 km = 5:00 per km, which equals 8:03 per mile, or 12.0 km/h (7.5 mph). At that pace, a 5K takes 25:00, a half marathon 1:45:29 and a marathon 3:30:59.",
    sections=[
        s("Pace and speed",
          "Pace is the time it takes to cover a unit of distance, usually a kilometre or a mile. Speed is the distance covered in a unit of time, usually an hour. They are two ways of describing the same thing. Runners mostly think in pace, because it maps directly onto splits and race plans: a 5:00 per km pace means passing each kilometre marker five minutes after the last.",
          "Converting is simple. Pace in minutes per km equals 60 divided by speed in km/h. A speed of 12 km/h is a pace of 60 ÷ 12 = 5:00 per km. One mile is 1.609 km, so pace per mile is pace per km × 1.609. A 5:00 per km pace is 8:03 per mile."),
        s("Worked examples",
          ("table", ["Distance and time", "Pace /km", "Pace /mi", "Speed"], [["5 km in 30:00", "6:00", "9:39", "10.0 km/h"], ["10 km in 50:00", "5:00", "8:03", "12.0 km/h"], ["Half marathon in 2:00:00", "5:41", "9:09", "10.5 km/h"], ["Marathon in 4:00:00", "5:41", "9:09", "10.5 km/h"]]),
          "A 2-hour half marathon and a 4-hour marathon happen to need the same pace, because the marathon is exactly twice the distance. For most runners, holding a 2-hour half marathon pace for the full marathon would be a major challenge."),
        s("Common pace targets",
          ("table", ["Goal", "Pace /km", "Pace /mi"], [["30-minute 5K", "6:00", "9:39"], ["25-minute 5K", "5:00", "8:03"], ["20-minute 5K", "4:00", "6:26"], ["1-hour 10K", "6:00", "9:39"], ["50-minute 10K", "5:00", "8:03"], ["2-hour half marathon", "5:41", "9:09"], ["4-hour marathon", "5:41", "9:09"], ["3:30 marathon", "4:59", "8:01"], ["Sub-3 marathon", "4:16", "6:52"]]),
          "Use these as reference points when setting goals. A target pace you have held in training for meaningful distances is far more realistic than one you have never run."),
        s("Why predicted race times are optimistic",
          "The table in the calculator multiplies your pace by each race distance. That assumes you could hold the same pace for every distance, which few runners can. Pace naturally slows as races get longer, because fatigue builds and energy stores fall. A runner who covers 10 km at 5:00 per km is more likely to run a half marathon at around 5:15 to 5:25 per km than at 5:00.",
          "For more realistic race predictions, the race time predictor on this site uses Riegel's formula, which adds time for longer distances. The pace calculator's table is best for answering a simpler question: if I run at this pace, what will my time be?"),
        s("Using pace in training",
          "Different sessions need different paces. Easy runs, which make up most of a runner's week, are usually 60 to 90 seconds per kilometre slower than 5K race pace, at a conversational effort. Threshold runs are a little slower than 10K pace. Intervals for VO2 max are around 3K to 5K pace. Long runs are often 30 to 90 seconds per kilometre slower than marathon goal pace.",
          ("ul", [
              "**Easy:** conversational, often 6:00 to 6:30 per km for a 25-minute 5K runner.",
              "**Threshold:** comfortably hard, around 5:10 to 5:20 per km.",
              "**Intervals:** hard, around 4:50 to 5:00 per km.",
              "**Long run:** relaxed, 6:00 to 6:45 per km.",
          ]),
          "Many runners run their easy days too fast, which leaves them too tired to hit the right pace on hard days. A pace calculator helps set and stick to the right numbers."),
        s("Pacing a race",
          "Even pacing, or a slightly faster second half, usually gives the best result. Starting too fast uses energy you'll need later, and the slow-down at the end costs more time than was gained. Break your target time into kilometre or mile splits, and aim to pass each marker within a few seconds of your target.",
          "Hills, wind and crowded starts all affect pace. On a hilly course, aim for even effort rather than even pace: slower uphill, faster downhill. In wind, run behind others where you can. In a crowded start, accept a slow first kilometre and make up the time gradually."),
        s("Treadmill pace and speed",
          "Treadmills usually show speed in km/h or mph. To run a 5:00 per km pace, set 12 km/h; for 6:00 per km, set 10 km/h. In miles, 8:00 per mile is 7.5 mph. Many runners set a 1% incline to better match the effort of running outdoors, where wind resistance and varied ground add a little work."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How do I calculate running pace?", "Divide your time by the distance. Running 10 km in 50 minutes is 5:00 per km, or 8:03 per mile."),
        ("How do I convert pace to speed?", "Divide 60 by your pace in minutes. A 5:00 per km pace is 60 ÷ 5 = 12 km/h."),
        ("What pace is a 25-minute 5K?", "5:00 per km, or 8:03 per mile."),
        ("What pace do I need for a 4-hour marathon?", "About 5:41 per km, or 9:09 per mile."),
        ("Why are my race predictions faster than my actual times?", "The pace calculator assumes you hold the same pace for every distance. Most runners slow down as distance increases."),
        ("What is a good running pace for beginners?", "Any pace that lets you run comfortably and keep a conversation. For many beginners, that is 6:30 to 8:00 per km or slower."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Race time predictor", "/tools/race-time-predictor"), ("Heart rate zone calculator", "/tools/heart-rate-zone-calculator"), ("Cooper test calculator", "/tools/cooper-test-calculator")],
)

D["rucking-calorie-calculator"] = base(
    "rucking-calorie-calculator",
    title="Rucking calorie calculator",
    h1="Rucking calorie calculator",
    description="Estimate calories burned rucking from body weight, pack load, pace, grade and terrain with the Pandolf load-carriage equation, versus the same walk unloaded.",
    intro="Enter your body weight, pack or vest load, walking pace, uphill grade, terrain and duration to estimate the calories you burn rucking, and how much more that is than the same walk without a load.",
    q="How many calories does rucking burn?",
    a="At 175 lb with a 30 lb pack, walking at 3 mph on flat pavement for an hour burns about 323 calories, according to the Pandolf equation. The same walk without the pack burns about 287, so the load adds about 13%. A 5% uphill grade raises the total to about 511 calories, and loose sand to about 560.",
    sections=[
        s("What rucking is",
          "Rucking means walking with a weighted backpack or vest. It comes from military training, where soldiers march long distances carrying heavy loads, and has become a popular form of fitness training. It raises the energy cost of walking, builds strength in the legs, hips, back and core, and improves aerobic fitness, all with much less impact than running.",
          "Its appeal is its simplicity. Most people can ruck: put some weight in a backpack, choose a route and walk. Load, pace, distance and terrain can all be adjusted to make it easier or harder, and it fits easily into daily life, whether as a commute, a dog walk or a weekend hike."),
        s("The Pandolf equation",
          "The calculator uses the load-carriage equation published by Kent Pandolf, Baruch Givoni and Ralph Goldman of the US Army Research Institute of Environmental Medicine in 1977. It predicts energy expenditure in watts from body mass, load, speed, grade and terrain:",
          ("ul", [
              "**M** = 1.5W + 2.0(W + L)(L ÷ W)² + η(W + L)(1.5V² + 0.35VG)",
              "**W** is body mass in kg, **L** the load in kg, **V** speed in m/s, **G** grade in %, and **η** a terrain factor.",
          ]),
          "The first term is the energy of standing. The second adds the cost of carrying the load, which rises with the square of the load relative to body weight. The third is the cost of moving, which rises with speed, grade, total mass and terrain. Watts are converted to calories at 1 watt = 0.01434 kcal per minute."),
        s("Terrain factors",
          ("table", ["Terrain", "Factor (η)"], [["Pavement or track", "1.0"], ["Dirt road", "1.1"], ["Trail or light brush", "1.2"], ["Heavy brush", "1.5"], ["Loose sand", "2.1"]]),
          "Soft or uneven ground makes every step harder, because energy is lost as the surface gives way and the body works to stay balanced. Loose sand roughly doubles the cost of moving compared with pavement."),
        s("Worked examples",
          "For a 175 lb (79.4 kg) person carrying 30 lb (13.6 kg) at 3 mph for 60 minutes:",
          ("table", ["Conditions", "Calories in 60 min", "Per minute"], [["Flat pavement", "323", "5.4"], ["Flat dirt road", "345", "5.7"], ["Flat trail", "366", "6.1"], ["5% grade, pavement", "511", "8.5"], ["10% grade, pavement", "699", "11.6"], ["Flat loose sand", "560", "9.3"]]),
          "Grade has the largest effect. A moderate 5% hill increases the burn by more than half, and a 10% hill more than doubles it. Increasing the load from 30 to 45 lb on flat pavement adds only about 22 calories an hour, while increasing pace from 3 to 4 mph adds about 168."),
        s("Load, pace or hills: what matters most",
          "Because the load term in the equation grows with the square of load relative to body weight, light loads add little and heavy loads add a lot. A pack that is 10 to 15% of body weight raises energy cost modestly. At 25 to 30%, the increase is much greater, but so is the strain on the back, hips and feet.",
          "Pace and grade affect total mass moving, so they raise energy cost for both body and load. For most people, the safest way to make rucking harder is to walk a little faster or add hills, rather than to keep adding weight."),
        s("Starting rucking safely",
          ("ul", [
              "**Start light:** about 10% of body weight, or 10 to 20 lb for most adults.",
              "**Start short:** 20 to 30 minutes on flat ground, two or three times a week.",
              "**Use a good pack:** with padded shoulder straps, a hip belt and the weight high and close to your back.",
              "**Wear supportive shoes:** walking or trail shoes with good grip.",
              "**Progress slowly:** increase time or distance first, then pace or hills, and load last.",
              "**Listen to your body:** stop if you feel pain in your back, knees, hips or feet.",
          ]),
          "Most military guidance recommends building load gradually over weeks, rarely by more than about 10% at a time. If you have back, knee or foot problems, get advice from a physiotherapist before starting."),
        s("Limits of the estimate",
          "The Pandolf equation was developed from studies of fit young soldiers walking steadily on treadmills and outdoor courses. It is less accurate for very slow or very fast walking, for downhill walking, which it doesn't model, and for people very different from the original study group. Later research has suggested it can underestimate energy cost at slow speeds.",
          "Treat the result as an estimate for comparing sessions, not an exact measurement. For example, it is useful for seeing how much more a hill route costs than a flat one, or how a heavier pack compares with a faster pace."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How many calories does rucking burn?", "At 175 lb with a 30 lb pack, walking at 3 mph on flat pavement burns about 323 calories an hour. Hills and soft terrain increase it substantially."),
        ("Does rucking burn more calories than walking?", "Yes. In the example above, the 30 lb pack adds about 13% compared with walking unloaded. Heavier loads and hills add more."),
        ("How heavy should my rucking pack be?", "Start at about 10% of body weight and build up gradually. Many people stay between 15 and 25%."),
        ("Is rucking better than running?", "It burns fewer calories per minute than running, but with much less impact, and it builds strength. Both have a place."),
        ("What is the Pandolf equation?", "A 1977 US Army equation that predicts the energy cost of walking with a load from body mass, load, speed, grade and terrain."),
        ("Does downhill rucking burn fewer calories?", "Usually, though steep descents still take effort. The Pandolf equation doesn't model downhill walking."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Interval walking calculator", "/tools/interval-walking-calculator"), ("Calories burned calculator", "/tools/calories-burned-calculator"), ("Steps to calories calculator", "/tools/steps-to-calories-calculator")],
)

D["workout-timer"] = base(
    "workout-timer",
    title="Workout timer",
    h1="Workout timer: EMOM, AMRAP, Tabata and interval timer",
    description="A free interval timer for EMOM, AMRAP, Tabata and custom work and rest intervals, with beeps, a countdown, fullscreen mode and space bar control.",
    intro="Choose EMOM, AMRAP, Tabata or a custom interval, set the numbers and press start. The timer counts each work and rest period, beeps at every change and in the last three seconds, and runs fullscreen.",
    q="How does the workout timer work?",
    a="Pick a mode and set the numbers. EMOM starts a new interval every minute for the minutes you choose. AMRAP counts down a single block. Tabata defaults to 20 seconds of work and 10 of rest for 8 rounds, 4 minutes in total. Custom lets you set any work, rest and rounds. Press start or the space bar, and the timer beeps at each change.",
    sections=[
        s("The four timer modes",
          ("table", ["Mode", "How it works", "Typical use"], [["EMOM", "A new interval starts every minute, on the minute", "Strength, skill work, controlled conditioning"], ["AMRAP", "One countdown; complete as many rounds as possible", "Circuits, conditioning tests"], ["Tabata", "20 s work, 10 s rest, 8 rounds by default", "Short, very hard intervals"], ["Custom", "Any work, rest and number of rounds", "Interval runs, circuits, rehab, walking intervals"]]),
          "Each mode suits a different kind of session. All of them run entirely in your browser, with no account or download."),
        s("EMOM: every minute on the minute",
          "In an EMOM, you start a set amount of work at the beginning of each minute, then rest for whatever time is left. If you finish 10 kettlebell swings in 25 seconds, you rest for 35. As fatigue builds, the work takes longer and the rest gets shorter, which makes EMOMs self-regulating: the harder you work, the less recovery you get.",
          "EMOMs work well for strength and skill practice, such as a set of heavy squats or a few pull-ups each minute, because they enforce consistent rest. They also suit conditioning, alternating exercises across minutes. A common format is 10 to 20 minutes, sometimes alternating two or three exercises: minute one swings, minute two push-ups, minute three rest, and repeat.",
          "Choose work that takes 20 to 40 seconds at the start. If you can't finish the work within the minute by the end, reduce the reps or load next time."),
        s("AMRAP: as many rounds as possible",
          "An AMRAP sets a fixed time and asks you to complete as many rounds of a circuit as you can. For example, a 12-minute AMRAP of 10 squats, 10 push-ups and 10 sit-ups. You record the rounds and extra reps completed, and that score becomes a benchmark to beat next time.",
          "AMRAPs are good for conditioning and for testing work capacity. Pacing matters: starting too fast leads to long rests later. Most people do best with a steady, sustainable effort, speeding up only in the last few minutes."),
        s("Tabata",
          "The Tabata protocol comes from a 1996 study by Izumi Tabata and colleagues at Japan's National Institute of Fitness and Sports. Trained athletes cycled at a very high intensity, about 170% of the power at their VO2 max, for 20 seconds, then rested for 10 seconds, repeated 8 times. After six weeks of training five days a week, both their aerobic and anaerobic capacity improved significantly.",
          "The original protocol was extremely hard, done on a bike at an intensity few people reach in everyday workouts. Today, Tabata is often used more loosely for any 20-on, 10-off interval set, with exercises like burpees, squats or kettlebell swings. Those sessions are still useful, but they rarely reach the intensity of the original study. The timer's default of 20, 10 and 8 rounds gives the classic 4-minute block, and all three numbers can be changed."),
        s("Custom intervals",
          "Custom mode lets you set any work and rest periods and any number of rounds. Some common examples:",
          ("ul", [
              "**Running intervals:** 60 s fast, 60 s easy, 10 rounds.",
              "**Circuit training:** 40 s work, 20 s rest, 6 rounds per circuit.",
              "**Japanese interval walking:** 180 s brisk, 180 s easy, 5 rounds.",
              "**Norwegian 4 × 4:** 240 s hard, 180 s easy, 4 rounds.",
              "**Back-to-back stations:** set rest to 0 to move straight from one station to the next.",
          ]),
          "The timer includes rest after every round, so total time is work plus rest times the number of rounds. For a 40-second work and 20-second rest set of 6 rounds, that is 6 minutes."),
        s("How to use the timer",
          ("ol", [
              "Choose a mode and set the numbers.",
              "Check the total time shown under the clock.",
              "Press Start, or the space bar, to begin. Press again to pause.",
              "Listen for the beeps: a higher tone starts work, a lower tone starts rest, and short beeps count down the last three seconds.",
              "Press Reset to start over, or change the settings to reset automatically.",
              "Use Fullscreen to make the clock easy to read across a room.",
              "Turn the sound off if you prefer silence.",
          ]),
          "While the timer runs, the page asks the browser to keep the screen awake, so your phone or laptop doesn't dim mid-workout. Some browsers don't support this; if yours dims, change your device's screen timeout before you start."),
        s("Choosing work and rest periods",
          "The ratio of work to rest shapes the session. Short work with long rest, such as 20 seconds on and 40 off, allows near-maximal effort each round and suits sprint and power work. Equal work and rest, such as 60 on and 60 off, suits hard aerobic intervals. Long work with short rest, such as Tabata's 20 on and 10 off, builds fatigue quickly and suits short, very intense blocks.",
          ("table", ["Goal", "Example work and rest", "Rounds"], [["Speed and power", "15 s work, 45 s rest", "6 to 10"], ["Aerobic intervals", "60 s work, 60 s rest", "8 to 12"], ["VO2 max", "3 to 4 min work, 2 to 3 min rest", "4 to 6"], ["Muscular endurance", "40 s work, 20 s rest", "6 to 8"], ["Very intense finisher", "20 s work, 10 s rest", "8"]]),
          "Choose the structure that fits the goal of the session, not only the one that feels hardest."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What is an EMOM workout?", "Every minute on the minute: you start a set amount of work at the start of each minute and rest for the remaining time. The timer beeps each new minute."),
        ("What is the difference between AMRAP and EMOM?", "An AMRAP is one countdown in which you complete as many rounds as possible. An EMOM restarts the work every minute, with rest built in."),
        ("How long is a Tabata workout?", "A classic Tabata is 8 rounds of 20 seconds work and 10 seconds rest, 4 minutes in total. Many sessions repeat several Tabata blocks."),
        ("Does the timer work offline and on my phone?", "It runs in your browser on phones, tablets and computers, with no account. Once the page has loaded, the timer itself doesn't need a connection."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Interval walking calculator", "/tools/interval-walking-calculator"), ("Heart rate zone calculator", "/tools/heart-rate-zone-calculator"), ("Workout plan builder", "/tools/workout-plan-builder")],
)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    from phase4_c_extra import EXTRA

    emit(D, EXTRA)
